  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nCurrently, upper level S/WV trough over the Northern Plains,\nwith associated light snow showers across the eastern Dakotas.\nUp to an inch of total snow remains possible across portions of\nsoutheastern North Dakota through early this evening, including\nacross the James River Valley. Web cam imagery does show some\naccumulations on the side of roads and in ditches, with no real\nimpacts expected. An area of low stratus clouds over central ND\ncontinues to slowly erode from the west, though this may \nstall/redevelop this evening as portrayed by some guidance \n(mainly RAP). \n\nUpper level trough morphs into a closed low tonight, which\nmomentarily stalls over eastern South Dakota. Elevated boundary\nlayer moisture and light winds tonight will likely result in fog\ndeveloping, with some dense fog possible. This is also \nindicated on hi-res guidance, though there are timing and aerial\nplacement differences between the RAP, HRRR, and RRFS. We opted\nto include this in the HWO, as headlines may be needed. S/WV mid\nlevel ridge then nudges into the region for Wednesday, and\nshould lead to decreasing cloud cover, fog clearing, and \ntemperatures warming into the mid 30s and 40s in the afternoon.\n\nA quasi-zonal flow is depicted for Thanksgiving Day, with a\nrelatively light southwesterly low level wind. Coupled with WAA\nand a mostly clear sky, a beautiful fall day awaits with\nafternoon temperatures well into the 40s to near 50.  \n\nS/WV trough develops east across far south central Canada on\nFriday, with west/northwesterly low level flow ramping up across\nthe state south of an area of sfc low pressure and ahead of a \ncold frontal boundary draped near the International Border. \nStrongest winds will be over far southwest ND, where wind \nheadlines may be needed Fri aft. Continued mild temperatures in\nthe 40s to lower 50s Fri aft, though the steady breeze will \nmake it feel colder. Low pressure continues into far northern \nMinnesota/southern Ontario by Saturday, with the cold FROPA \nmoving south through the state Friday night. In the wake of the \nfront, Saturday will be blustery and colder, along with perhaps \nsome light snow showers. Right now NBM and our official forecast\nis dry, and would expect this to change in future forecast \niterations. Another embedded S/WV quickly follows, moving \nsoutheast across south central Canada into northern MN Sat \nnight-Sun. This feature drives a secondary cold front through \nthe region, trending temperatures colder into Sunday and maybe \nanother shot for a few light snow showers.",
